## Why This Is Asked
Assesses ability to design a tiny, deterministic routing loop for an offline-first finance tool, with explicit module boundaries and safety signals.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_privacy, offline_capable
- Edge-case handling: no_cache, conflicting signals
- Module boundaries: TransactionFilter, SummaryComposer, LocalCacheManager
- Deterministic payload construction

## Code Example
```javascript
// Tiny router example (pseudo)
function routePrompt(prompt, offline) {
  const signals = extractSignals(prompt);
  const module = offline ? 'LocalCacheManager' : 'NetworkFetch';
  return {module, signals};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test cache-miss scenarios and signal conflicts?
- How to extend to multi-account data and multiple currencies?

## Why This Is Asked

Gauges practical, client-side prompt evaluation with safety and tone controls for high-stakes brands. Focuses on a beginner-friendly rubric and a concrete routing prototype, suitable for scalable chat systems.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt scoring rubrics
- Client-side routing logic
- Safety and bias considerations
- Minimal unit testing for NLP-ish logic

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(input){
  const lower = input.toLowerCase();
  const clarity = /order|status|refund/.test(lower) ? 4 : 2;
  const safety = !lower.includes('password') ? 4 : 2;
  const bias = /(racial|gender|biased)/.test(lower) ? 5 : 1;
  if (clarity >= 4 && safety >= 4 && bias <= 2) return 'concise';
  if (lower.includes('please') || lower.includes('sorry')) return 'empathetic';
  return 'authoritative';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you extend scoring to multilingual prompts?
- How would you evaluate and mitigate bias in edge prompts?
- How would you test the classifier with real user prompts?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests ability to design a lightweight, repeatable prompt-testing workflow and catch prompt drift early.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt versioning
- Drift metrics (embedding similarity, content guards)
- Reproducibility and logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal prototype skeleton for drift check
function driftFlag(basePrompt, paraphrase) {
  const baseOut = model(basePrompt)
  const paraOut = model(paraphrase)
  const sim = cosineSimilarity(embed(baseOut), embed(paraOut))
  return sim < 0.8
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend this for multi-language prompts?
- How would you integrate with CI to prevent drift before deploy?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant policy enforcement, guard-detection, and prompt rewriting with auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant policy registry and per-tenant constraints
- Guard-detection vs. prompt rewriting
- System guard injection and explicit privacy constraints
- Audit artifacts and drift/telemetry considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const policy = {
  Tesla: { pii: true, retentionDays: 90 },
  Google: { pii: false, retentionDays: 365 },
  Plaid: { pii: true, retentionDays: 30 }
};
function guardAndRewrite(tenant, prompt) {
  const violations = [];
  const lower = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (lower.includes("password") || lower.includes("ssn")) {
    violations.push("PII-like content");
  }
  const rewritten = `[${tenant}] ${prompt} /* guard: ensure policy compliance */`;
  return { rewritten, violations };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate determinism and drift for two tenants under evolving policies?  
- What tests would you add to catch policy-evading prompts without compromising latency?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical ability to enforce data policies in prompt processing, not just theory. It probes edge-case handling and testability.

## Key Concepts
- Prompt safety layering
- Data_sensitivity tagging
- Policy-driven guard rails and auditing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal guard example
function guardPrompt(prompt, policy){
  const intent = parseIntent(prompt);
  const data = tagSensitivity(prompt, policy);
  if(!allowed(data, policy)){ throw new Error('Policy violation'); }
  const guarded = applyGuard(prompt, policy);
  const check = `compliant:${Boolean(guarded)}`;
  return {guardedPrompt: guarded, check};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model conflicting signals (privacy vs analytics) and resolve them?
- How would you test for prompt leakage across multi-hop prompts?
